Progress: 3 of 4 pass. The untrusted client now gets... let me see the failure detail — probably got a response instead of handshake error? Wait, earlier this subtest passed when handshake failed. Now with a proper server cert maybe the untrusted client cert... hmm, maybe the failure is "expected TLS handshake failure ... got status XXX". Which status? Possibly 403 fallback — meaning server didn't reject the untrusted cert?! That would be a security issue in my understanding of verify_if_given. Or maybe the client didn't send the cert because the server's acceptable CA list doesn't include untrusted CA — Go client only sends certificates if... no, Go client sends the configured certificate regardless? Actually Go client selects client cert based on server's CertificateRequest acceptable CAs; if no match, it sends empty certificate (unless... ). Yes! Go's client: if none of the configured certs chain to the server's acceptable CAs, it sends no certificate. So untrusted cert never gets sent → treated as no cert → 403 fallback.

Earlier (first run) the "untrusted" subtest passed because the server had no cert at all — every request failed at handshake, including that one. False positive.

So how to test chain-3 rejection? Options:
- Configure client to always send: Go client does send cert if `Certificates` is set and server requests... The logic: `getClientCertificate` returns first configured cert if no acceptable CAs specified or if it matches? Let me recall crypto/tls client handshake: it calls `c.config.getClientCertificate(&CertificateRequestInfo{AcceptableCAs: ...})`. The default implementation: if len(Certificates)==1 return it? Actually default: it tries to find a cert whose chain matches AcceptableCAs; "If no acceptable certificate is found, the client will send... " — the default getClientCertificate returns `Certificate{}` (empty) when no match? From memory of crypto/tls code: 

```go
func (c *Config) getClientCertificate(...) {
    if c.GetClientCertificate != nil { ... }
    for _, cert := range c.Certificates {
        if err := cri.SupportsCertificate(&cert); err == nil { return &cert }
    }
    return new(Certificate) // empty
}
```

Hmm, I believe SupportsCertificate checks acceptable CAs and signature schemes. If no match → empty cert → no client cert sent. So the untrusted cert isn't sent. To force sending, set `GetClientCertificate` on the client to always return the untrusted cert. Then server (verify_if_given) will verify → fail → handshake error. 

Let me update httpClientWithCert to set GetClientCertificate returning the first chain. Simpler: in the untrusted subtest, build transport manually with GetClientCertificate.

Actually simpler: make httpClientWithCert always set GetClientCertificate to return the first configured cert. That mimics clients that always send. But for no-cert case, keep empty.

I'll modify: in httpClientWithCert, after appending certs, set:
```go
transport.TLSClientConfig.GetClientCertificate = func(*tls.CertificateRequestInfo) (*tls.Certificate, error) {
    return &chains[0].cert, nil
}
```
when len(chains) > 0.

